WEBVTT


00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:07.070
A knitter wanted to knit the body
of a kid's cardigan in one piece.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.380
She planned to have a design along the
hem and she wanted to avoid side seams.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.930
Here are the steps for combining the
back and front of a child's sweater.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:21.890
Remember because of needle bed
limitations, this will only apply to

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:23.510
smaller size sweaters.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:28.910
Create the basic shape of the
cardigan in Standard Garment Shaping.

00:00:29.865 --> 00:00:34.215
Save your design and then open
it in Original Pattern Drafting.

00:00:36.225 --> 00:00:43.305
Select the pieces and right click, drag,
and move them into the correct position.

00:00:48.845 --> 00:00:53.375
Notice that there are three
points along each side seam.

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:59.730
To make things easier, let's use the
delete tool and remove that extra point

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:00.269
for now.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:06.179
With one front active,
select the delete tool.

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:12.630
Choose the point you want to remove
from that active piece and delete it.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:18.660
Turn off the tool, select the next
piece, which would be the back.

00:01:19.289 --> 00:01:20.670
Turn on the tool.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.350
Delete the points.

00:01:23.475 --> 00:01:29.685
Turn off the tool, select the next
front piece, turn on the tool.

00:01:32.815 --> 00:01:34.045
Delete the point.

00:01:39.935 --> 00:01:41.285
Turn off the tool.

00:01:43.425 --> 00:01:47.835
The feature we're going to
be using is >Piece and >Join.

00:01:51.085 --> 00:01:54.685
Look closely, with the
pieces aligned this way.

00:01:54.685 --> 00:01:59.455
If I joined them, I'll be adding width
to the body, which isn't what I want.

00:02:01.964 --> 00:02:07.274
So I'll select the left piece and drag
it to align it with the back piece.

00:02:08.894 --> 00:02:12.975
Notice that there's a red dotted line
indicating that they're in place.

00:02:14.715 --> 00:02:20.265
The currently selected piece is light
gray, and the back piece is dark gray.

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:23.295
They are still two
separate pieces right now.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.390
We'll click >Piece and >Join.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:34.060
Notice that the cursor turns to a plus
sign, making it easier for you to select

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:34.570
the points.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:42.100
Click on the top point of the selected
piece, and then click again to select the

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.860
point of the dark gray piece behind.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.810
Scroll down to the
lower points and repeat.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:53.310
Click and click.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:01.710
Notice, now that both pieces are light
gray, so we've joined them, but you're not

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:02.280
finished.

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.870
To complete the join, you need to click
anywhere away from that new piece you

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:07.590
created.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:14.640
Let's do it on the other side, right click
on that other front piece and drag it over

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.130
till it aligns with the back side seam.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.380
Notice the red dotted line.

00:03:20.985 --> 00:03:24.765
From the menu select >Piece and >Join.

00:03:25.665 --> 00:03:28.215
Click, click on the top two points.

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:36.045
Click, click on the bottom, two points,
the piece is now, all the same color.

00:03:37.245 --> 00:03:39.195
But notice these two symbols.

00:03:39.345 --> 00:03:41.325
You still aren't quite done.

00:03:41.745 --> 00:03:45.705
Don't forget to click away
from the piece to finish.

00:03:46.665 --> 00:03:54.144
And we now have one piece fronts and backs
that we can cast on and get knitting.

